      "title": "Sertraline",
      "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/druginfo/meds/a697048.html?utm_source=mplusconnect&utm_medium=service",
      "summary": "Sertraline is used to treat depression, obsessive-compulsive disorder (bothersome thoughts that won't go away and the need to perform certain actions over and over), panic attacks (sudden, unexpected attacks of extreme fear and worry about these attacks), posttraumatic stress disorder (disturbing psychological symptoms that develop after a frightening experience), and social anxiety disorder (extreme fear of interacting with others or performing in front of others that interferes with normal life). It is also used to relieve the symptoms of premenstrual dysphoric disorder, (mood swings, irritability, bloating, and breast tenderness). Sertraline is in a class of antidepressants called selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs). It works by increasing the amounts of serotonin, a natural substance in the brain that helps maintain mental balance."
    },
    {
      "title": "Antidepressants",
      "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/antidepressants.html?utm_source=mplusconnect&utm_medium=service",
      "summary": "<h3>What are antidepressants?</h3>\n<p>Antidepressants are prescription medicines to treat depression. <a href=\"https://medlineplus.gov/depression.html?utm_source=mplusconnect\">Depression</a> is more than feeling a little sad or \"blue\" for a few days. It's a very common, serious medical illness that affects your mood and general <a href=\"https://medlineplus.gov/mentalhealth.html?utm_source=mplusconnect\">mental health</a>. It can make you feel tired, hopeless, worried, or fearful. It can change your thinking, sleeping, and eating. Depression may make some people think about ending their lives.</p>\n<p>But antidepressants can help many people who have depression. Researchers think antidepressants may help improve the way your brain uses certain chemicals that control mood or stress.</p> \n\n<h3>Are antidepressants used for other conditions?</h3>\n<p>A health care provider may prescribe antidepressants for <a href=\"https://medlineplus.gov/anxiety.html?utm_source=mplusconnect\">anxiety</a>, <a href=\"https://medlineplus.gov/chronicpain.html?utm_source=mplusconnect\">chronic pain</a>, or <a href=\"https://medlineplus.gov/insomnia.html?utm_source=mplusconnect\">insomnia</a>. Sometimes providers al"
    }
